The local Rust measurement module adapts the OS observation sequence and
unit conversions from crates/pq-bench-support/src/lib.rs. It adds explicit
unavailable results and checked conversions. One separate final process
record reports the lifetime RSS peak through that snapshot; primitive
records retain endpoint RSS only. It does not install the
research allocator or import the support crate's statistics implementation.
The Python reporter follows that source's nearest-rank percentile rule,
with new record validation and separate treatment of failures, warmups,
unavailable metrics, and censored observations.

The research attribution files trace the earlier sampling, percentile,
atomic-output, completion-state, and build-provenance design to
jonwalch/zcash-pq-bench at 6424563c6be763047f737bc922e8980e28f0b834,
specifically src/measurement.rs and build.rs, under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
That is retained transitive attribution. Those original files are not
direct imports into this framework. The research notices identify the
OS observation code as originating in the research prototype.

The Python build/run and provenance modules selectively adapt the research
measurement driver and existing product acceptance helpers. They retain
source and binary identities, bounded JSON handling, completion state,
and raw evidence. The isolated primitive command flow, strict process
supervision, run registration, and failure records are benchmark adaptations.

Primitive cases call the existing product signer and verifier APIs.
Their crypto code and source/license notices remain in their original
crates. A focused test consumes the existing public v5-1of1 fixture
without generating or modifying it. Benchmark fixture inputs and CLI
validation are new glue; they are not independent cryptographic references.

Wallet payment cases additionally reuse unchanged product WatchStore APIs and
adapt the product's synthetic wallet test sources and classical P2SH fixture
helpers at e51290264ebaa52f224c8fd994545e321226ecfc. The research four-family
payment experiment at 53a6e4bd1c11d90349fd3d989507f0e1a4995112 informs the
foreground/component timer and independent artifact-check boundaries. Its
alternate backends, memory inventory model and historical fee are not imported.
The product wallet's actual enrollment, approval, journal and fee rules apply.

The payment correctness process reuses existing independent transaction and
wallet format oracles. Its existing SLH Python loader is LGPL-3.0-or-later and
uses separately pinned reference Python and portable C BLAKE3 sources; licenses
remain with those sources and in tests/slh-dsa-b128s/reference. These test-only
references are not linked into the Rust node, wallet or benchmark executable.
The bounded Python secp256k1 public-signature checker is new independent code;
it is not a general Script interpreter or a secret-key implementation.
See sources.json for exact paths, hashes and adaptations. Prepared reference
source, license and library snapshots belong to run artifacts, not product source.

The offline node adapter uses event and metric meanings from the existing
Zakura digest, trace emitter, block-application path, and mempool gauges.
Its strict event correlation and import validation are new Python code.
Linux process/cgroup/interface collection and the proposed propagation
event adapter are new measurement code. No existing live propagation
instrumentation is claimed by that adapter.

The registered node collector reuses these process/cgroup readers and mempool
adapters, the existing bounded process supervisor and report contract. Its static
node-build validation selectively adapts the existing acceptance receipt checks;
it preserves compiled source identity separately from benchmark instrumentation.
Process/configuration binding, bounded loopback HTTP collection, raw-observation
replay and attach-only lifecycle tests are new integration code. It does not
launch nodes or supply funded workloads, latency hooks or chain acceptance.
The separate benchmark node-build wrapper calls the unchanged acceptance build
helper and reuses the benchmark compiler/configuration provenance reader. It
retains the original acceptance receipt and adds build-time settings for matched
comparison. Legacy acceptance receipts remain readable without claiming those
missing settings. HTTP deadline enforcement adapts the standard-library parser's
I/O boundary; no HTTP parser or transport implementation is imported.
Node comparison identities separate the default/SLH build variant from matched
observation conditions. End-to-end comparison tests adapt the existing mocked
build, sampler and report fixtures, while retaining historical configuration IDs.
Portable receipt validation preserves historical build command paths and checks
current artifact bytes. Mempool evidence retains exact required metric lines
for replay and a whole-response hash/size, avoiding unrelated scrape output.

No alternate cryptographic backend, research node overlay, historical
result set, research checkout, or research Git history is a runtime
dependency. Attribution does not establish benchmark acceptance or permit
an agent to launch a human-only validation workload.
